1
00:00:02,833 --> 00:00:04,600
The most famous trip home in history

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:05,833
took about ten years.

3
00:00:06,167 --> 00:00:08,367
He spent eight of them as a houseguest:

4
00:00:08,367 --> 00:00:10,433
seven years with the nymph Calypso,

5
00:00:10,433 --> 00:00:11,800
one with the witch Circe.

6
00:00:12,133 --> 00:00:13,667
And when he finally got home,

7
00:00:13,667 --> 00:00:15,067
a hundred and eight houseguests

8
00:00:15,067 --> 00:00:16,167
were eating his food.

9
00:00:20,167 --> 00:00:21,967
Hi, and welcome to LearnLoop,

10
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:24,133
where old stories loop back to your life.

11
00:00:25,133 --> 00:00:26,533
This is Homer's Odyssey,

12
00:00:26,533 --> 00:00:28,933
a poem about 2,700 years old.

13
00:00:29,267 --> 00:00:31,733
Troy has just fallen after ten years of war.

14
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:34,200
Odysseus, king of the island of Ithaca,

15
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,267
sets sail for home with twelve ships.

16
00:00:37,267 --> 00:00:39,400
Waiting at home are his wife, Penelope,

17
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:41,367
and a son who was a baby when he left.

18
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:43,033
It should be a quick trip.

19
00:00:43,367 --> 00:00:44,100
It is not.

20
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,300
The monsters come from one dinner party,

21
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:49,633
where Odysseus tells the story himself.

22
00:00:49,967 --> 00:00:51,233
So this is his version.

23
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:54,233
Early on, his men walk into the cave

24
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:56,533
of a giant Cyclops called Polyphemus.

25
00:00:56,867 --> 00:00:58,033
He traps them inside

26
00:00:58,033 --> 00:01:00,233
and eats six of the crew, two at a time.

27
00:01:00,567 --> 00:01:03,067
Odysseus tells the giant his name is Nobody.

28
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,367
That night, while the giant sleeps,

29
00:01:05,367 --> 00:01:06,667
they blind him with a stake

30
00:01:06,667 --> 00:01:07,767
hardened in the fire.

31
00:01:08,767 --> 00:01:09,967
He screams for help,

32
00:01:09,967 --> 00:01:12,000
and his neighbors ask who is hurting him.

33
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:13,467
Nobody, he shouts.

34
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,300
So they shrug and go home.

35
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:17,000
At dawn, the men escape

36
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:18,467
under the bellies of his rams.

37
00:01:19,467 --> 00:01:21,867
A perfect escape, until Odysseus ruins it.

38
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:24,267
From his ship, he shouts his real name,

39
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:25,167
his father's name,

40
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:26,300
and his home island.

41
00:01:26,633 --> 00:01:28,967
History's first case of sharing your location

42
00:01:28,967 --> 00:01:30,067
with the wrong person.

43
00:01:31,067 --> 00:01:33,100
The Cyclops is a son of Poseidon,

44
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:34,067
god of the sea.

45
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:35,767
He prays to his father:

46
00:01:35,767 --> 00:01:38,033
let Odysseus get home late, alone,

47
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:39,333
on someone else's ship.

48
00:01:39,667 --> 00:01:41,467
Poseidon won't kill Odysseus,

49
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:43,067
but he'll keep him away from home.

50
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:45,033
And between here and Ithaca,

51
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:46,267
there is a lot of sea.

52
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,100
From here on, every stop goes wrong.

53
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:51,500
The keeper of the winds

54
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,267
gives him a bag

55
00:01:52,267 --> 00:01:54,200
with the storm winds tied inside.

56
00:01:54,533 --> 00:01:56,700
For nine days they sail straight home,

57
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:59,167
and with Ithaca in sight, he falls asleep.

58
00:02:00,167 --> 00:02:02,367
His crew thinks the bag is full of gold.

59
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:03,467
They open it,

60
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:05,400
and the winds blow them all the way back.

61
00:02:05,733 --> 00:02:06,767
Recalculating.

62
00:02:07,767 --> 00:02:09,200
Next, a tribe of giants

63
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:10,900
smashes every ship but his.

64
00:02:11,233 --> 00:02:12,667
Twelve ships left Troy.

65
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Now there is one.

66
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,133
On the next island,

67
00:02:16,133 --> 00:02:17,967
Circe turns his men into pigs.

68
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:19,767
Pig bodies, human minds.

69
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:21,833
Hermes gives Odysseus a herb

70
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:23,033
that blocks her magic.

71
00:02:23,367 --> 00:02:24,067
It works.

72
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:25,833
He stays a year anyway.

73
00:02:26,833 --> 00:02:28,067
Then come the Sirens.

74
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:29,933
In Homer there are just two,

75
00:02:29,933 --> 00:02:31,667
and what they promise is knowledge.

76
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,500
Odysseus plugs his crew's ears with wax

77
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:36,300
and has himself tied to the mast.

78
00:02:36,633 --> 00:02:38,167
Noise-cancelling for the crew,

79
00:02:38,167 --> 00:02:39,833
a live concert for the captain.

80
00:02:40,833 --> 00:02:43,067
Next, a strait with two bad options.

81
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:45,467
Scylla, a monster with six heads,

82
00:02:45,467 --> 00:02:47,067
or Charybdis, a whirlpool

83
00:02:47,067 --> 00:02:49,300
that swallows the sea three times a day.

84
00:02:49,633 --> 00:02:51,233
Scylla takes six of his men.

85
00:02:52,233 --> 00:02:53,867
On the island of the sun god,

86
00:02:53,867 --> 00:02:56,167
the starving crew eats his sacred cattle.

87
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,733
Zeus sinks the ship with a thunderbolt,

88
00:02:58,733 --> 00:03:00,467
and only Odysseus survives.

89
00:03:01,467 --> 00:03:03,500
He washes up on Calypso's island.

90
00:03:03,833 --> 00:03:05,467
She keeps him for seven years,

91
00:03:05,467 --> 00:03:07,067
and offers to make him immortal.

92
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:08,333
He says no.

93
00:03:08,667 --> 00:03:09,800
He wants to go home,

94
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:11,700
where his wife has a problem of her own.

95
00:03:13,533 --> 00:03:15,667
So Odysseus is stuck on one island.

96
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,600
Penelope is stuck on another.

97
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:19,933
A hundred and eight men want to marry her,

98
00:03:19,933 --> 00:03:21,900
and they won't leave until she picks one.

99
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:25,433
Meanwhile, they party in the palace every day,

100
00:03:25,433 --> 00:03:27,500
on his cattle, his sheep and his wine.

101
00:03:27,833 --> 00:03:29,367
They eat everything in sight,

102
00:03:29,367 --> 00:03:31,500
and they'd still give the place one star.

103
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:33,633
Penelope stalls.

104
00:03:33,967 --> 00:03:35,733
She'll choose a husband, she says,

105
00:03:35,733 --> 00:03:37,367
once she finishes weaving a shroud

106
00:03:37,367 --> 00:03:38,667
for Odysseus's father.

107
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,233
She weaves it by day

108
00:03:40,233 --> 00:03:42,167
and secretly unpicks it by night.

109
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:43,467
For three years.

110
00:03:44,467 --> 00:03:46,567
In the fourth year, a maid gives her away.

111
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:49,300
Meanwhile, their son, the baby from the start,

112
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:50,067
is grown up.

113
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,733
He sails off to find news of his father,

114
00:03:52,733 --> 00:03:54,900
guided by an old friend called Mentor.

115
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:58,000
Mentor is really Athena in disguise,

116
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,367
and that's where our word mentor comes from.

117
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:03,400
The suitors plan to ambush him on his way home.

118
00:04:03,733 --> 00:04:05,833
Now Penelope is out of excuses,

119
00:04:05,833 --> 00:04:07,500
the suitors are out of patience,

120
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:09,200
and Odysseus is out of ships.

121
00:04:11,033 --> 00:04:13,567
The gods finally order Calypso to let him go.

122
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,533
A kingdom of sailors carries him

123
00:04:15,533 --> 00:04:17,467
the rest of the way, fast asleep.

124
00:04:18,467 --> 00:04:19,967
Twenty years after he left,

125
00:04:19,967 --> 00:04:21,200
Odysseus is home.

126
00:04:21,533 --> 00:04:23,633
Late, alone, on someone else's ship.

127
00:04:23,967 --> 00:04:26,133
Exactly what the Cyclops asked for.

128
00:04:26,467 --> 00:04:27,367
You have arrived.

129
00:04:28,367 --> 00:04:30,567
Athena disguises him as an old beggar,

130
00:04:30,567 --> 00:04:32,233
so he can scout his own house.

131
00:04:32,567 --> 00:04:35,133
His old dog, Argos, knows him at once,

132
00:04:35,133 --> 00:04:37,600
wags his tail one last time, and dies.

133
00:04:37,933 --> 00:04:40,400
His old nurse knows him by a scar on his leg.

134
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:42,967
Then Penelope sets a test.

135
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,267
Whoever strings Odysseus's old bow

136
00:04:45,267 --> 00:04:47,333
and shoots an arrow through twelve axes

137
00:04:47,333 --> 00:04:48,400
will be her husband.

138
00:04:48,733 --> 00:04:50,233
The suitors try and fail.

139
00:04:50,567 --> 00:04:53,000
The beggar strings it as easily as a lyre,

140
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:54,733
and shoots through all twelve.

141
00:04:55,067 --> 00:04:56,267
Then the rags come off,

142
00:04:56,267 --> 00:04:58,567
and the suitors' very long party is over.

143
00:04:59,567 --> 00:05:01,167
Penelope still isn't sure,

144
00:05:01,167 --> 00:05:03,300
so she asks a servant to move their bed.

145
00:05:03,633 --> 00:05:06,567
Odysseus is outraged: nobody can move that bed.

146
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:08,800
One of its posts is an olive tree,

147
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:10,167
still rooted in the ground.

148
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:12,433
Only the real Odysseus would know that.

149
00:05:12,767 --> 00:05:15,000
She has out-tricked the master trickster.

150
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,300
When the suitors' families come for revenge,

151
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:20,533
Athena makes both sides swear peace.

152
00:05:20,867 --> 00:05:22,067
The poem ends there.

153
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:23,467
The story doesn't.

154
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:26,833
It moved into our language.

155
00:05:27,167 --> 00:05:29,233
A long, hard journey is an odyssey,

156
00:05:29,233 --> 00:05:32,033
a meaning English has used since the late 1800s.

157
00:05:32,367 --> 00:05:34,100
Stuck between two dangers?

158
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:35,967
You're between Scylla and Charybdis.

159
00:05:36,967 --> 00:05:39,500
In 2017, Emily Wilson published

160
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:41,533
the first English translation of the poem

161
00:05:41,533 --> 00:05:42,300
by a woman.

162
00:05:42,633 --> 00:05:45,533
Her first line: Tell me about a complicated man.

163
00:05:45,867 --> 00:05:47,633
The Greek word means, roughly,

164
00:05:47,633 --> 00:05:49,033
a man of many turns.

165
00:05:50,033 --> 00:05:51,967
This summer, Christopher Nolan's film

166
00:05:51,967 --> 00:05:54,767
took in about 1.77 billion dollars.

167
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:57,700
And by mid-July, before it even opened,

168
00:05:57,700 --> 00:05:59,200
US print sales of the poem

169
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,467
were up 76 percent year to date.

170
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:04,033
Just know the film skips the Nobody trick,

171
00:06:04,033 --> 00:06:06,167
and on screen, Athena is the only god.

172
00:06:07,167 --> 00:06:08,833
Quick game: real or fake?

173
00:06:09,167 --> 00:06:11,467
One: the Trojan Horse is in the Odyssey.

174
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:14,300
Two: Odysseus's dog recognizes him

175
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:15,400
after twenty years.

176
00:06:15,733 --> 00:06:18,167
Three: Homer says the Cyclops has one eye

177
00:06:18,167 --> 00:06:19,400
in the middle of his forehead.

178
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:23,733
The fake is number three.

179
00:06:24,067 --> 00:06:26,300
Homer only mentions his eye, singular,

180
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:27,733
and never says where it is.

181
00:06:28,067 --> 00:06:29,800
The forehead comes from Hesiod,

182
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:31,533
describing different Cyclopes.

183
00:06:31,867 --> 00:06:33,000
The horse is real.

184
00:06:33,333 --> 00:06:35,100
It's in the Odyssey three times,

185
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:36,433
and never in the Iliad.

186
00:06:37,433 --> 00:06:39,633
So why did a trip home take ten years?

187
00:06:39,967 --> 00:06:41,800
One boast, one angry sea god,

188
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:43,200
and some very long visits.

189
00:06:43,533 --> 00:06:45,267
Next time you post where you are,

190
00:06:45,267 --> 00:06:46,333
think of Odysseus.

191
00:06:47,333 --> 00:06:49,333
But why was he at Troy in the first place?

192
00:06:49,667 --> 00:06:50,967
Blame a golden apple.

193
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:53,400
That's a later myth, and our next video.

194
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:55,633
Thanks for watching.

195
00:06:55,633 --> 00:06:57,033
Subscribe so you don't miss it,

196
00:06:57,033 --> 00:06:58,067
and see you next time.

